<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad Haji Ibrahim Egal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1928">1928</a>–<a href="/years/2002">2002</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad Haji Ibrahim Egal was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> politician who became prime minister of the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a> at independence in June 1960 and later prime minister of the Somali Republic from 1967 until the 1969 coup. In 1993 clan elders at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borama">Borama</a> chose him as president of the self-declared Republic of Somaliland, which he led until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-haji-ibrahim-egal.jpg" alt="Muhammad Haji Ibrahim Egal" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_Haji_Ibrahim_Egal_1968_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Egal was born on 15 August 1928 at Odweyne in the British Somaliland Protectorate, the son of a wealthy merchant. He belonged to the Issa Musse division of the Habar Awal, part of the larger Isaaq clan. He completed primary, intermediate and secondary schooling in British Somaliland and was then sent to the United Kingdom to continue his studies, which was unusual at the time. He married Asha Saeed Abby, and they had five children, three sons and two daughters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>On 26 June 1960 Egal became prime minister of the newly independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>, which merged five days later with the former Trust Territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> to form the Somali Republic on 1 July 1960. He served the republic as minister of defence from 1960 to 1961 and minister of education from 1962 to 1963. In 1967 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdirashid-ali-shermarke">Abdirashid Ali Shermarke</a> appointed him prime minister. His rise was reportedly financed largely by covert support from the American Central Intelligence Agency to him and other pro-Western members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-youth-league">Somali Youth League</a>. In 1968 he publicly said that Somalia would not drop its aim of uniting all Somalis, but he provoked popular anger when he later gave up claims to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogaden">Ogaden</a>.</p><p>Shermarke was assassinated on 15 October 1969, while Egal was in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. A Supreme Revolutionary Council led by Major General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siad-barre">Siad Barre</a> seized power, renamed the country the Somali Democratic Republic, banned political parties and dissolved parliament. Egal was detained, released, and served as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1976 to 1978, before the Barre government imprisoned him again on charges of conspiracy until 1985.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As second president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>, from 1993 until his death, Egal disarmed and rehabilitated rebel groups and introduced the Somaliland shilling, a passport and a national flag. In 1995 he wrote to Israeli Prime Minister Yitzhak Rabin seeking diplomatic ties; Abdirahman Tuur, the first president, said Egal hoped this would help win recognition from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Hardliners in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-national-movement">Somali National Movement</a> doubted his commitment to separation, and in August 2001 he survived by one vote a motion charging him with pursuing it half-heartedly.</p><p>Egal died on 3 May 2002 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, during surgery at a military hospital. After a state funeral he was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbera">Berbera</a> beside his father, with about 4,000 mourners reported, and parliament declared seven days of mourning. Dahir Rayale Kahin was sworn in as president the next day.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad Haji Ibrahim Egal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The SNM chairman Abdirahman Ahmed Ali Tuur became the first president, and a second conference at Borama in 1993 transferred power to <b>Muhammad Haji Ibrahim Egal</b>, a former prime minister of the Somali Republic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-somaliland">Republic of Somaliland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>British Somaliland received independence on 26 June 1960 as the State of Somaliland, with a legislative assembly elected earlier that year and <b>Muhammad Haji Ibrahim Egal</b> as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-somaliland">State of Somaliland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Odweine. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=9.41,45.06&amp;year=1976" aria-label="Odweine on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Muhammad Haji Ibrahim Egal"><image href="/maps/globes/g06000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3754 4199h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3754 4199h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=9.41,45.06&amp;year=1976">What was here in 1976? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
